  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Definition
    
    Gift-custom is the Aurora Stack's governing norm requiring that public prosperity — shared resources, successful harvests, completed constructions — be ceremonially announced and displayed before private accumulation. Surplus from crystal-forging runs by the ☽'ofund' Guild, fire-core shipments secured through The Icebound Exchange, or completed spire construction is carried to the ☽'sanctmocsaecus' Twilight-bells and proclaimed publicly before household distribution.
    
    The custom has no formal enforcement mechanism. It has never needed one.
    
    ## Origins
    
    Qingoqa aihua'Mist-wrought, the Aurora Stack's festival herald, codified Gift-custom during the first winter of the Frozen Peace, but the practice predated her formalization. Ice-memory from the late Thaw period records sharing behaviors among tunnel survivors — ration-splitting, fire-tenders yielding warm shifts to the injured — that already carried the custom's essential logic: scarcity makes hoarding suicidal.
    
    Gift-custom formally commemorates two events: the Thaw itself, when cooperative distribution was arithmetic survival; and the succession crisis known as Of The First Shelf, when competing claims nearly shattered the Aurora Stack until guild cooperation proved more durable than individual authority.
    
    ## Enforcement and Continuity
    
    During the Orca Incursion, penguins who hoarded supplies were not executed. They were left behind when the colony relocated, their names scratched from memorial-ice. The punishment is social erasure — not violence but silence. Whether this constitutes mercy or something worse is a question the custom does not invite.
    
    The most revealing test came after the Tide Fall Blasphemy, a Wake-Singer desecration-ritual that drowned three Aurora Stack outposts. With no surplus to distribute, the ceremonies continued regardless. The Twilight-bells rang through empty spires. An anonymous account from the period describes a solitary figure lifting a crystal before no audience at all. The custom constrains in absence as firmly as in plenty: the performance does not pause when there is nothing left to share.
    
    This is what distinguishes Gift-custom from law. Law requires something to regulate. Gift-custom requires only the willingness to perform regulation, even into the void.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
